.TH NVIM2 1 "November 2026" "nvim2 1.0" "User Commands"
.nh
.SH NAME
nvim2 \- Neovim wrapper that records every launch for frecency ranking
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nvim2
.RI [ nvim-options ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B nvim2
.RI [ nvim-options ]
.I query
.RI [ query ...]
.B \-\-find
.br
.B nvim2 \-\-init
.BR bash | zsh
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nvim2
records each launch in a history file, then runs
.BR nvim (1)
with the same arguments.
Each file named on the command line gets one history line with the date, its absolute path and the arguments as typed.
A launch without a file records the current folder as a dashboard entry.
.PP
Neovim options are passed through unchanged.
Arguments starting with
.B \-
or
.B +
are not treated as files, and neither is the value following an option that takes one
.RB ( \-c ,
.BR \-\-cmd ,
.BR \-u ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-q ,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-w ,
.BR \-W ,
.BR \-S ,
.BR \-\-listen ,
.BR \-\-startuptime ).
Every argument after
.B \-\-
is treated as a file.
.PP
The history is read by
.BR nvimi (1),
which ranks files by frecency and opens them.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-find
Treat the non-option words as a search query instead of file names.
.B nvim2
opens the existing file from the history with the highest frecency score whose path matches the query, and records that launch.
Matching is case-insensitive, the words must appear in the path in the order given, and the last word must appear in the file name.
If nothing matches, an error is printed and Neovim is not started.
.TP
.BR "\-\-init bash" | zsh
Print shell code that binds Alt-Enter to run the current command line with
.B \-\-find
appended, when the line starts with
.B nvim
or
.BR nvim2 .
Load it from the shell startup file with
.BR "eval \(dq$(nvim2 \-\-init bash)\(dq" .
.TP
.B \-\-vim
Run
.BR vim (1)
instead of
.BR nvim .
The launch is recorded in the same history.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration file contains one
.I "key = value"
pair per line, with a space on each side of the equals sign.
Values are whole numbers.
Empty lines and lines starting with
.B #
are ignored.
If the file is missing, a warning is printed and the defaults are used.
.TP
.BI limit " (default 10000)"
Number of history lines kept after a trim.
.TP
.BI wait " (default 1000)"
Number of lines allowed past
.I limit
before the next trim.
.TP
.BI rows " (default 15)"
Maximum number of rows in the
.BR nvimi (1)
picker.
.TP
.BI halflife_days " (default 7)"
Half-life of the frecency score, in days.
.SH SCORING
Each recorded launch adds 0.5^(age/half-life) to the score of its file, where age is the time elapsed since that launch.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/nvim2/nvim2.config
Configuration file.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/nvim2/history_nvim.csv
History file.
One line per launch with three tab-separated fields: local date and time, absolute path (or the current folder), and the arguments as typed (or
.B NO_ARG
for a launch without a file).
Once the file is larger than about 200 * (limit + wait) bytes and holds more than limit + wait lines, the oldest lines are removed so that the newest
.I limit
lines remain.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Used to locate the configuration and history files.
.B nvim2
exits with an error if it is not set.
.SH EXIT STATUS
On success
.B nvim2
is replaced by Neovim, so the exit status is that of Neovim.
Otherwise:
.TP
.B 1
.B HOME
is not set, the history file cannot be opened for writing,
.B \-\-find
was given without a query or found no match, or
.B \-\-init
was given an unsupported shell.
.TP
.B 127
Neovim could not be started.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B nvim2 main.cpp
Open and record
.IR main.cpp .
.TP
.B nvim2 \-R notes.md
Open read-only; the option reaches Neovim unchanged.
.TP
.B nvim2 conway cgof \-\-find
Open the best matching file from the history, for example
.IR ~/projects/conwaygof/cgof.cpp .
.TP
.B alias nvim=nvim2
Record every
.B nvim
typed in the shell.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nvimi (1),
.BR nvim (1)
